  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 125 AM Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Seasonable and drier conditions persist \nthrough Wed night with unseasonably hot weather returning late \nweek into the weekend.\n\nA few days of near zero rain chances is expected as a highly \nanomalous and potentially record breaking mid/upper lvl \nsubtropical ridge (+2.5 to 3 standardized height anomalies from \n850 to 200mb Tues through Thurs night) builds over the TN Valley\nand southern Appalachians; perhaps even reaching 600 dam at H5 \nby Wed. Thankfully, strong convergence aloft on the backside of \na shortwave to our northeast will favor building high pressure \nover the Northeast and ridging down into the Carolinas into \nWednesday, supporting a brief dip down to seasonable \ntemperatures (not-as-hot). However, this will not last as \nsurface ridging begins to weaken over the western Atlantic and \nbring a moderating trend back to 10-15 degrees above normal into\nthe weekend.\n\nNWP ensemble guidance is coming into better agreement in the \ndevelopment of a an area of mid/upper-lvl low pressure \ndeveloping off the Southeast coast, sliding west underneath the \nsubtropical ridge and then stalling somewhere near the northern\nGulf coast this weekend. As is typically the case with cutoff \nlows, forecast uncertainty is high on their evolution and when \nthey will be picked up by any approaching troughs, but it does \nappear this feature may play a role in bringing more widespread \nprecipitation chances to the area next week.",
